Article 4: When hearing a domain name dispute case, the People's Court shall determine that the defendant's registration, use, or other acts constitute infringement or unfair competition if the following conditions are met:
(1) The civil rights and interests for which the plaintiff requests protection are lawful and valid;
(2) The defendant's domain name or its principal part constitutes a reproduction, imitation, translation, or transliteration of the plaintiff's well-known trademark; or is identical or similar to the plaintiff's registered trademark, domain name, etc., sufficient to cause misidentification among the relevant public;
(3) The defendant has no rights or interests in the domain name or its principal part, and has no legitimate reason for registering or using the domain name;
(4) The defendant's registration and use of the domain name are in bad faith.

Article 5: Where any of the following circumstances is proven with respect to the defendant's conduct, the People's Court shall determine that the defendant acted in bad faith:
(1) Registering another person's well-known trademark as a domain name for commercial purposes;
(2) Registering or using a domain name that is identical or similar to the plaintiff's registered trademark, domain name, etc., for commercial purposes, intentionally causing confusion with the products, services, or website provided by the plaintiff, and misleading Internet users to visit its website or other online sites;
(3) Previously having offered to sell, rent, or otherwise transfer the domain name at a high price to obtain improper benefits;
(4) After registering the domain name, neither using it nor preparing to use it, but intentionally preventing the right holder from registering the domain name;
(5) Having other bad-faith circumstances.
Where the defendant proves that, before the occurrence of the dispute, the domain name held by it had already acquired a certain degree of reputation and could be distinguished from the plaintiff's registered trademark, domain name, etc., or has other circumstances sufficient to prove that it did not act in bad faith, the People's Court may determine that the defendant did not act in bad faith.
